  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "LIMITED",
    "confidence": 89,
    "conclusion": "The available evidence supports semantic stability under exact repetition: no anchor changed semantic class across five identical runs. Observable responses can still vary in broader state, but some apparent instability is difficult to interpret because reliability was only moderate or weak for several component labels and poor for exact referent agreement. Prompt paraphrasing, rather than nominally identical repetition, reliably increased full-state variation for most anchors while preserving semantic class; affected dimensions included claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. The proposed explanation that repetition or accumulation alone automatically hardens weak premises should be rejected for this cohort, as no eligible trajectory showed preregistered premise hardening. Retain a distinction between stable semantic conclusions and condition-sensitive surface or policy-state features, while treating claims about noisy dimensions cautiously."
  },
